    "sourcing": "Rich. The Library holds his letters and his treatise On the Holy Spirit in English. It holds his Ascetic Works, with the Longer and Shorter Rules, and his Address to Young Men. It also holds his Hexaemeron homilies and his three books Against Eunomius in Greek and Latin.\n\nSome famous works are disputed. Letter 38, often used to show that the three Cappadocians agreed, may be by Basil of Caesarea or by his brother Gregory of Nyssa.",
    "floorNote": "His faith is not in question.",
-   "relationsSummary": "Told within the Cappadocians, with his brother Gregory of Nyssa and his friend Gregory of Nazianzus. He wrote On the Holy Spirit against those who ranked the Spirit below the Father and the Son. He made his brother and his friend bishops, and the friend resented it for years.",
-   "why": "Basil was bishop of Caesarea in Cappadocia from about 370. His treatise On the Holy Spirit answered those who ranked the Spirit below the Father and the Son. He argued from how the church already prayed. A council in 787 told double monasteries to keep his rule, and later churches call him \"the Great\".",
+   "relationsSummary": "Told within the Cappadocians, with his brother Gregory of Nyssa and his friend Gregory of Nazianzus. He wrote On the Holy Spirit against those who ranked the Spirit below the Father and the Son.",
+   "why": "Basil was bishop of Caesarea in Cappadocia. His treatise On the Holy Spirit answered those who ranked the Spirit below the Father and the Son. He argued from how the church already prayed. In the sixth century Benedict of Nursia pointed his monks to Basil's rule as an example of good living, and later churches call him \"the Great\".",
    "entry": null,
-   "teaser": "Caesarea in Cappadocia, c. 329 to 379 CE: a bishop who defended the Spirit's place beside the Father and the Son, and wrote a rule for monks that a council and Benedict still named.",
-   "longDescription": "Years later, defending himself in a letter, Basil of Caesarea looked back. He had wasted nearly all his youth, he wrote, in the vain labor of learning a wisdom God had made foolish. \"Then one day, like a man roused from deep sleep, I turned my eyes to the wonderful light of the truth of the gospel.\"\n\nThat waking set the course of his life in Caesarea, an inland city in Asia Minor. Basil gave up teaching rhetoric for a life of prayer and shared work. He wrote rules that taught monks to live together and serve one another. He built a great lodging for the poor and the sick a little way outside the city. As bishop from 370, he defended the Holy Spirit's place beside the Father and the Son. He died in 378 or 379, before a council confessed it.\n\nHe taught rhetoric, the art of public speaking, the historian Rufinus says. Who turned him away? In his own telling, he read in the gospel of selling goods and sharing with the poor, and went looking for monks. His brother, Gregory of Nyssa, gives the credit to their sister Macrina. Rufinus says his friend Gregory of Nazianzus took him from the teacher's chair.\n\nIn Pontus, near the Black Sea, Basil founded a community and led it, Gregory says. His rules were answers to his brothers' questions, and one asks whether to live alone or with others. Love does not seek its own good, Basil answers, and Christ washed his disciples' feet. \"Whom, then, will you wash? Whom will you care for? Behind whom will you be last, if you live by yourself?\" A man alone has no one to serve.\n\nThe bishop of Caesarea made him a priest, though they quarreled. When the emperor Valens, who favored the party that ranked the Son below the Father, moved against the churches that held them equal, Basil came back to serve the bishop.\n\nWhen the old bishop died, Gregory says, Basil was chosen against the bishops of the province. The emperor's highest official ordered him to accept the emperor's faith, on pain of losing his goods, exile, torture and death. Gregory's speech in Basil's memory is praise. In his telling, Basil answered that a man who owns only rags and a few books cannot be robbed, and that death would only send him sooner to God. A rival bishop once blocked his road with armed men. Basil answered by making his brother bishop of Nyssa and his friend bishop of Sasima, a hurt, Gregory said, that time had not healed.\n\nAccused before the governor, Basil wrote: whom does it harm to build a lodging for strangers, travelers and the sick, with doctors and transport? The rich filled it at Basil's urging, Gregory says. Lepers driven from their towns were taken in, and Basil greeted them as brothers and tended them himself, Gregory says.\n\nBasil closed prayers with praise to the Father with the Son, together with the Holy Spirit, and at other times through the Son, in the Holy Spirit. Some attacked these as new, contradictory words. The Spirit, they held, could not be ranked with the Father and the Son. He answered from what the church already did, and in a letter put it this way: \"As we were baptized, so we confess our faith. As we confess our faith, so too we give praise.\" Gregory admits that in writing Basil held back the bare words that the Spirit is God, from caution, though he often preached it.\n\nIn 381, after his death, a council at Constantinople confessed the Spirit worshipped and glorified with the Father and the Son. Rufinus put his Rule into Latin in 397 or 398. In the sixth century Benedict of Nursia closed his own Rule by pointing his monks to Basil's as an example of good living. In 787 a council told the houses still standing where men and women lived as monks, side by side but apart, to keep his rule.\n\nGregory says the whole city poured around Basil as he died.",
+   "teaser": "Caesarea in Cappadocia, fourth century CE: a bishop who defended the Spirit's place beside the Father and the Son, and wrote a rule for monks that Benedict still named.",
+   "longDescription": "Years later, defending himself in a letter against men who, he wrote, were spreading lies about him, Basil of Caesarea looked back. He had wasted nearly all his youth, he wrote, in the vain labor of learning a wisdom God had made foolish, the learning of this world. \"Then one day, like a man roused from deep sleep, I turned my eyes to the wonderful light of the truth of the gospel.\"\n\nThat waking set the course of his life in Caesarea, an inland city in Asia Minor, in the fourth century. Basil gave up teaching rhetoric, the art of public speaking, for a life of prayer and shared work. He wrote rules that taught monks to live together and serve one another, and he built a great lodging for the poor and the sick a little way outside the city. As its bishop, he defended the Holy Spirit's place beside the Father and the Son, and he died before a council, a meeting of bishops, declared it in its creed.\n\nThe old writers differ on what turned him. In his own telling, he read in the gospel of selling goods and sharing with the poor. His brother, Gregory of Nyssa, says their sister Macrina turned him, while the historian Rufinus says his friend Gregory of Nazianzus drew him away from teaching.\n\nIn Pontus, near the Black Sea, Basil founded a community and led it. His rules were answers to his brother monks' questions, and one asks whether to live alone or with others. Love does not seek its own good, Basil answers, and Christ washed his disciples' feet, but a man alone has no one to serve. \"Whom, then, will you wash? Whom will you care for? Behind whom will you be last, if you live by yourself?\"\n\nThe bishop of Caesarea made him a priest, but they quarreled, and Basil went back to Pontus. When the emperor Valens, who favored the party that ranked Christ, the Son, below God the Father, moved against the churches that held them equal, Basil came back to serve the bishop.\n\nWhen the old bishop died, Basil was made bishop, though the bishops of the province fought it out of envy, Gregory of Nazianzus says in his speech in Basil's memory. The emperor's highest official ordered him to accept the emperor's faith, or else lose his goods and face exile, torture and death. In Gregory's telling, Basil answered that a man who owns only rags and a few books cannot be robbed, and that death would only send him sooner to God. When the province was divided, a rival bishop claimed part of his churches and once blocked his road with armed men. Basil answered by making more bishops for the province, Gregory says.\n\nHis accusers had won over the governor, and Basil wrote to ask what harm the state suffered from him, or from building a lodging for strangers, travelers and the sick, with doctors and transport. The rich gave to it at his urging. Lepers driven from their towns were taken in, and Basil greeted them as brothers and tended them himself.\n\nBasil closed prayers by giving glory to the Father in two forms: with the Son, together with the Holy Spirit, and through the Son, in the Holy Spirit. The first set the Spirit beside the Father and the Son. Some attacked these as new, contradictory words, holding that the Spirit could not be ranked with the Father and the Son. He answered in a book, On the Holy Spirit, that the church already baptized new believers in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit together. A letter of his puts the same thought in two lines: \"As we were baptized, so we confess our faith. As we confess our faith, so too we give praise.\" Gregory says that Basil, out of caution, held back from writing in plain words that the Spirit is God, though he often preached it.\n\nThe whole city poured around Basil as he died. In 381, after his death, a council at Constantinople declared in its creed that the Spirit is worshipped and glorified with the Father and the Son. Rufinus put his works, his rules for monks among them, into Latin. In the sixth century Benedict of Nursia closed his own Rule by pointing his monks to Basil's as an example of good living.",
