-    <div class="reading"><p>Years later, defending himself in a letter, Basil of Caesarea looked back. He had wasted nearly all his youth, he wrote, in the vain labor of learning a wisdom God had made foolish. &quot;Then one day, like a man roused from deep sleep, I turned my eyes to the wonderful light of the truth of the gospel.&quot;</p><p>That waking set the course of his life in Caesarea, an inland city in Asia Minor. Basil gave up teaching rhetoric for a life of prayer and shared work. He wrote rules that taught monks to live together and serve one another. He built a great lodging for the poor and the sick a little way outside the city. As bishop from 370, he defended the Holy Spirit&#039;s place beside the Father and the Son. He died in 378 or 379, before a council confessed it.</p><p>He taught rhetoric, the art of public speaking, the historian Rufinus says. Who turned him away? In his own telling, he read in the gospel of selling goods and sharing with the poor, and went looking for monks. His brother, Gregory of Nyssa, gives the credit to their sister Macrina. Rufinus says his friend Gregory of Nazianzus took him from the teacher&#039;s chair.</p><p>In Pontus, near the Black Sea, Basil founded a community and led it, Gregory says. His rules were answers to his brothers&#039; questions, and one asks whether to live alone or with others. Love does not seek its own good, Basil answers, and Christ washed his disciples&#039; feet. &quot;Whom, then, will you wash? Whom will you care for? Behind whom will you be last, if you live by yourself?&quot; A man alone has no one to serve.</p><p>The bishop of Caesarea made him a priest, though they quarreled. When the emperor Valens, who favored the party that ranked the Son below the Father, moved against the churches that held them equal, Basil came back to serve the bishop.</p><p>When the old bishop died, Gregory says, Basil was chosen against the bishops of the province. The emperor&#039;s highest official ordered him to accept the emperor&#039;s faith, on pain of losing his goods, exile, torture and death. Gregory&#039;s speech in Basil&#039;s memory is praise. In his telling, Basil answered that a man who owns only rags and a few books cannot be robbed, and that death would only send him sooner to God. A rival bishop once blocked his road with armed men. Basil answered by making his brother bishop of Nyssa and his friend bishop of Sasima, a hurt, Gregory said, that time had not healed.</p><p>Accused before the governor, Basil wrote: whom does it harm to build a lodging for strangers, travelers and the sick, with doctors and transport? The rich filled it at Basil&#039;s urging, Gregory says. Lepers driven from their towns were taken in, and Basil greeted them as brothers and tended them himself, Gregory says.</p><p>Basil closed prayers with praise to the Father with the Son, together with the Holy Spirit, and at other times through the Son, in the Holy Spirit. Some attacked these as new, contradictory words. The Spirit, they held, could not be ranked with the Father and the Son. He answered from what the church already did, and in a letter put it this way: &quot;As we were baptized, so we confess our faith. As we confess our faith, so too we give praise.&quot; Gregory admits that in writing Basil held back the bare words that the Spirit is God, from caution, though he often preached it.</p><p>In 381, after his death, a council at Constantinople confessed the Spirit worshipped and glorified with the Father and the Son. Rufinus put his Rule into Latin in 397 or 398. In the sixth century Benedict of Nursia closed his own Rule by pointing his monks to Basil&#039;s as an example of good living. In 787 a council told the houses still standing where men and women lived as monks, side by side but apart, to keep his rule.</p><p>Gregory says the whole city poured around Basil as he died.</p></div><p class="quotes-note">Quotations are given in our modern English. The printed wording of each is below.</p><details class="quotes-panel"><summary>Quotations and references</summary><p class="source-group">Quotations</p><ul class="sources-list"><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“Then one day, like a man roused from deep sleep, I turned my eyes to the wonderful light of the truth of the gospel.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“Then once upon a time, like a man roused from deep sleep, I turned my eyes to the marvellous light of the truth of the Gospel”<br><span class="source-note">Basil, Letter 223.2, tr. Blomfield Jackson, in Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, second series, vol. 8</span><br><span class="source-note">The Greek of the letter is not held, so the English stands alone. The modern English keeps Jackson&#039;s order and gives once upon a time as one day, and marvellous as wonderful.</span></li><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“Whom, then, will you wash? Whom will you care for? Behind whom will you be last, if you live by yourself?”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“Τίνα οὖν ἀπονίψεις ; τίνα θεραπεύσεις ; τίνος ἔσχατος ἔσῃ, αὐτὸς καθ᾽ ἑαυτὸν διάγων ;”<br><span class="source-note">Basil, Longer Rule 7, Greek of the Patrologia Graeca, vol. 31</span><br><span class="source-note">The Greek has whom, with no word for feet. Clarke&#039;s English adds whose feet from the sentence before, where Christ washes the feet of the disciples. The modern English follows the Greek, and the narrator gives the feet as the plain sense.</span></li><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“As we were baptized, so we confess our faith. As we confess our faith, so too we give praise.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“As we were baptized, so we profess our belief.  As we profess our belief, so also we offer praise.”<br><span class="source-note">Basil, Letter 159.2, tr. Blomfield Jackson, in Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, second series, vol. 8</span><br><span class="source-note">The Greek of the letter is not held, so the English stands alone. The modern English gives profess our belief as confess our faith, and so also as so too.</span></li></ul><p class="source-group">References</p><ul class="sources-list"><li>Letter 223.2, tr. Blomfield Jackson, placed in 375: the opening, his own account of wasting his youth and waking to the gospel, written in self-defense against Eustathius of Sebaste.</li><li>Rufinus, Church History 11.9: he taught rhetoric, and Gregory of Nazianzus took him from the teacher&#039;s chair to a monastery. Gregory of Nyssa, Life of Macrina: credits their sister Macrina. Letter 223.2: in his own telling he read in the gospel of selling goods and sharing with the poor, and went looking for monks.</li><li>Gregory of Nazianzus, Oration 43.29: he founded a community in Pontus and led it. Oration 43.34: his friend calls the rules legislation for the monastic life. Longer Rule 7: whether to live alone or with others, and that love does not seek its own good. The Greek of Longer Rule 7: the quotation about washing and being last.</li><li>Oration 43.28-31 and 43.34: the bishop of Caesarea made him a priest, the quarrel, and his return when the emperor Valens, who favored the party that ranked the Son below the Father, moved against the churches.</li><li>Oration 43.37, 43.48-50, 43.58-59: chosen against the bishops of the province, the emperor&#039;s highest official and his threats, the rival bishop who blocked his road with armed men, and the new bishops of Nyssa and Sasima. The speech is Gregory&#039;s, in praise.</li><li>Letter 94, to the governor Elias, placed in 372: whom it harms to build a lodging for strangers, travelers and the sick, with doctors and transport. Oration 43.63: the rich filled it at his urging. Oration 43.63: the lepers taken in and greeted as brothers.</li><li>On the Holy Spirit 1.3: the two forms of praise and the attack on them as new and contradictory. On the Holy Spirit 10.24: the opponents&#039; view that the Spirit could not be ranked with the Father and the Son. Letter 159.2, placed about 373: the quotation on baptism, faith and praise. Oration 43.68-69: Gregory says Basil held back the bare words in writing from caution and often preached it.</li><li>First Council of Constantinople, 381: the Spirit worshipped and glorified with the Father and the Son, in the tree&#039;s own entry. Fremantle&#039;s note on Rufinus: the Rule put into Latin in 397 or 398. Benedict&#039;s Rule, ch. 73: Basil&#039;s Rule as an example of good living. Canon 20 of the Second Council of Nicaea, 787: the double monasteries still standing.</li><li>Oration 43.79: the whole city poured around him as he died.</li><li>World census, Cappadocian Christianity: his tradition.</li></ul></details>
+    <div class="reading"><p>Years later, defending himself in a letter against men who, he wrote, were spreading lies about him, Basil of Caesarea looked back. He had wasted nearly all his youth, he wrote, in the vain labor of learning a wisdom God had made foolish, the learning of this world. &quot;Then one day, like a man roused from deep sleep, I turned my eyes to the wonderful light of the truth of the gospel.&quot;</p><p>That waking set the course of his life in Caesarea, an inland city in Asia Minor, in the fourth century. Basil gave up teaching rhetoric, the art of public speaking, for a life of prayer and shared work. He wrote rules that taught monks to live together and serve one another, and he built a great lodging for the poor and the sick a little way outside the city. As its bishop, he defended the Holy Spirit&#039;s place beside the Father and the Son, and he died before a council, a meeting of bishops, declared it in its creed.</p><p>The old writers differ on what turned him. In his own telling, he read in the gospel of selling goods and sharing with the poor. His brother, Gregory of Nyssa, says their sister Macrina turned him, while the historian Rufinus says his friend Gregory of Nazianzus drew him away from teaching.</p><p>In Pontus, near the Black Sea, Basil founded a community and led it. His rules were answers to his brother monks&#039; questions, and one asks whether to live alone or with others. Love does not seek its own good, Basil answers, and Christ washed his disciples&#039; feet, but a man alone has no one to serve. &quot;Whom, then, will you wash? Whom will you care for? Behind whom will you be last, if you live by yourself?&quot;</p><p>The bishop of Caesarea made him a priest, but they quarreled, and Basil went back to Pontus. When the emperor Valens, who favored the party that ranked Christ, the Son, below God the Father, moved against the churches that held them equal, Basil came back to serve the bishop.</p><p>When the old bishop died, Basil was made bishop, though the bishops of the province fought it out of envy, Gregory of Nazianzus says in his speech in Basil&#039;s memory. The emperor&#039;s highest official ordered him to accept the emperor&#039;s faith, or else lose his goods and face exile, torture and death. In Gregory&#039;s telling, Basil answered that a man who owns only rags and a few books cannot be robbed, and that death would only send him sooner to God. When the province was divided, a rival bishop claimed part of his churches and once blocked his road with armed men. Basil answered by making more bishops for the province, Gregory says.</p><p>His accusers had won over the governor, and Basil wrote to ask what harm the state suffered from him, or from building a lodging for strangers, travelers and the sick, with doctors and transport. The rich gave to it at his urging. Lepers driven from their towns were taken in, and Basil greeted them as brothers and tended them himself.</p><p>Basil closed prayers by giving glory to the Father in two forms: with the Son, together with the Holy Spirit, and through the Son, in the Holy Spirit. The first set the Spirit beside the Father and the Son. Some attacked these as new, contradictory words, holding that the Spirit could not be ranked with the Father and the Son. He answered in a book, On the Holy Spirit, that the church already baptized new believers in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit together. A letter of his puts the same thought in two lines: &quot;As we were baptized, so we confess our faith. As we confess our faith, so too we give praise.&quot; Gregory says that Basil, out of caution, held back from writing in plain words that the Spirit is God, though he often preached it.</p><p>The whole city poured around Basil as he died. In 381, after his death, a council at Constantinople declared in its creed that the Spirit is worshipped and glorified with the Father and the Son. Rufinus put his works, his rules for monks among them, into Latin. In the sixth century Benedict of Nursia closed his own Rule by pointing his monks to Basil&#039;s as an example of good living.</p></div><p class="quotes-note">Quotations are given in our modern English. The printed wording of each is below.</p><details class="quotes-panel"><summary>Quotations and references</summary><p class="source-group">Quotations</p><ul class="sources-list"><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“Then one day, like a man roused from deep sleep, I turned my eyes to the wonderful light of the truth of the gospel.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“Then once upon a time, like a man roused from deep sleep, I turned my eyes to the marvellous light of the truth of the Gospel”<br><span class="source-note">Basil, Letter 223.2, tr. Blomfield Jackson, in Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, second series, vol. 8</span><br><span class="source-note">The Greek of the letter is not held, so the English stands alone. The modern English keeps Jackson&#039;s order and gives once upon a time as one day, and marvellous as wonderful.</span></li><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“Whom, then, will you wash? Whom will you care for? Behind whom will you be last, if you live by yourself?”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“Τίνα οὖν ἀπονίψεις ; τίνα θεραπεύσεις ; τίνος ἔσχατος ἔσῃ, αὐτὸς καθ᾽ ἑαυτὸν διάγων ;”<br><span class="source-note">Basil, Longer Rule 7, Greek of the Patrologia Graeca, vol. 31</span><br><span class="source-note">The Greek has whom, with no word for feet. Clarke&#039;s English adds whose feet from the sentence before, where Christ washes the feet of the disciples. The modern English follows the Greek, and the narrator gives the feet as the plain sense.</span></li><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“As we were baptized, so we confess our faith. As we confess our faith, so too we give praise.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“As we were baptized, so we profess our belief.  As we profess our belief, so also we offer praise.”<br><span class="source-note">Basil, Letter 159.2, tr. Blomfield Jackson, in Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, second series, vol. 8</span><br><span class="source-note">The Greek of the letter is not held, so the English stands alone. The modern English gives profess our belief as confess our faith, and so also as so too.</span></li></ul><p class="source-group">References</p><ul class="sources-list"><li>Letter 223.2, tr. Blomfield Jackson: the opening, his own account of wasting his youth and waking to the gospel, written in self-defense against Eustathius of Sebaste. The same letter, lines 39105-39113: the slanderers sullying his name among the brethren; lines 39133-39137: the uselessness of the wisdom of this world.</li><li>Rufinus, Church History 11.9: he taught rhetoric, and Gregory of Nazianzus took him from the teacher&#039;s chair to a monastery. Gregory of Nyssa, Life of Macrina: credits their sister Macrina. Letter 223.2: in his own telling he read in the gospel of selling goods and sharing with the poor.</li><li>Gregory of Nazianzus, Oration 43.29: he founded a community in Pontus and led it. Oration 43.34: his friend calls the rules legislation for the monastic life. Longer Rule 7: whether to live alone or with others, and that love does not seek its own good. The Greek of Longer Rule 7: the quotation about washing and being last.</li><li>Oration 43.28-31 and 43.34: the bishop of Caesarea made him a priest, the quarrel, and his return when the emperor Valens, who favored the party that ranked the Son below the Father, moved against the churches.</li><li>Oration 43.37, 43.48-50, 43.58-59: chosen against the bishops of the province, the emperor&#039;s highest official and his threats, the province divided, the rival bishop who claimed part of his churches and blocked his road with armed men, and the new bishops for the province. The speech is Gregory&#039;s, in Basil&#039;s memory.</li><li>Letter 94, to the governor Elias: his accusers, and what harm the government suffers from him; whom it harms to build a lodging for strangers, travelers and the sick, with doctors and transport. Oration 43.63: the rich filled it at his urging. Oration 43.63: the lepers taken in and greeted as brothers.</li><li>On the Holy Spirit 1.3: the two forms of praise and the attack on them as new and contradictory. On the Holy Spirit 10.24: the opponents&#039; view that the Spirit could not be ranked with the Father and the Son. On the Holy Spirit 27, lines 14165-14170: the answer from baptism, that they must teach him not to baptize as he received, or not to believe as he was baptized, or not to give glory as he believed. Letter 159.2: the quotation on baptism, faith and praise. Oration 43.68-69: Gregory says Basil held back the bare words in writing from caution and often preached it.</li><li>First Council of Constantinople, 381: the Spirit worshipped and glorified with the Father and the Son, in the tree&#039;s own entry; the creed&#039;s text in The Seven Ecumenical Councils, lines 13393-13396. Rufinus&#039;s preface to his Latin Rule, for the abbot Urseius, and Gennadius 17: his works, the rules for monks among them, put into Latin. Benedict&#039;s Rule, ch. 73: Basil&#039;s Rule as an example of good living.</li><li>Oration 43.79: the whole city poured around him as he died.</li><li>World census, Cappadocian Christianity: his tradition.</li></ul></details>
