s monks kept it.</li><li>The Second Council of Nicaea, in 787, decreed that no new double monastery be built, and that the double monasteries already in existence keep the rule of Basil and be ordered by his precepts, with monks and nuns not dwelling together.</li><li>Sozomen, about sixty-five years after Basil&#039;s death, calls the Basileias the most celebrated hospice for the poor, and says Basil, bishop of Caesarea, set it up and that it took its name from him and still keeps it.</li><li>The First Council of Constantinople, in 381, after his death, confessed the Spirit worshipped and glorified with the Father and the Son. No held source says the council took up his teaching.</li><li>Theodoret, in his Church History, calls the see of Caesarea the see of the great Basil, a light of the world, and in the Eranistes gives a testimony of the holy Basil among his authorities.</li><li>Photius read twenty-five books by Theodore of Antioch against Eunomius in defense of St. Basil, and an attack on Eunomius by Sophronius, also in defense of St. Basil. Jerome says Basil himself wrote books against Eunomius.</li><li>Gregory of Nyssa, in a letter to his brother Peter that opens his books against Eunomius, says that when Basil died the controversy with Eunomius passed to him, and that he wrote in part to defend Basil&#039;s good name against Eunomius&#039;s abuse.</li><li>Palladius says Evagrius of Pontus was ordained reader by the holy Basil, bishop of Caesarea.</li><li>The tree&#039;s own entry for his tradition says his rules for communal ascetic life remain foundational for Eastern Orthodox monasticism.</li></ul><h3>How they're remembered</h3><ul><li>Gregory of Nazianzus says he and Basil were at Athens together, like two branches of one river, and that on Basil&#039;s return the city of Caesarea took possession of him as a second founder and patron.</li><li>In his letter against Eustathius, Basil says he went to many monks in Alexandria and the rest of Egypt, in Palestin