rs. He says the new bishops were Basil&#039;s answer, and that he himself was treated as an appendage to the scheme, a cause of pain which even time has not obliterated. Socrates says Gregory was first made bishop of Sasima, one of the cities of Cappadocia.</li><li>Gregory says the lepers, driven from their cities, homes and families, were taken into the new city, and that Basil saluted them as brothers and kissed them.</li><li>Gregory says that Basil, dying, ordained the best of his attendants so that his disciples would not be defrauded of the priesthood. At his funeral, Gregory says, the market places, porticos and houses were full, Jews, Greeks and foreigners mourned with his own people, and in the crush many died.</li><li>Sozomen says Basil was a great admirer of Ephrem the Syrian and was astonished at his learning.</li></ul></section><section><h2>What Is Uncertain</h2><ul><li>Who turned him from rhetoric. Basil&#039;s own letter, written in self-defense against Eustathius of Sebaste, tells of his waking to the gospel and of the monks he sought out. His brother Gregory of Nyssa, in the Life of his sister, credits Macrina. Rufinus, about twenty-three years after Basil&#039;s death, credits Gregory of Nazianzus, who took him from the teacher&#039;s chair to a monastery. Basil teaching rhetoric rests on Rufinus.</li><li>The date of his death. No held old source gives the year. Jerome says only that he died in the reign of Gratian.</li><li>The Rules. Their date is not held. Sozomen reports that some said Eustathius of Sebaste wrote the ascetic treatises commonly attributed to Basil.</li><li>Gregory of Nazianzus&#039;s account is a speech in Basil&#039;s memory, so it is praise. The famine, the dialogue with the emperor&#039;s official, the lepers and the funeral are told in his voice, and Sozomen gives a shorter version of only the official&#039;s threat.</li><li>Who made him bishop. Gregory, who was there, says bishops brought from a distance, his own father among