-    <div class="reading"><p>Epiphanius called his book the Panarion, which means a medicine chest. In its preface he says what it is for. &quot;We are going to show you the names of the heresies. We will uncover their unholy deeds, which are like venom and poison. Alongside them we will fit antidotes: cures for those who have been bitten, and protection for those who may yet fall into it.&quot;</p><p>A heresy, in his book, is a teaching the church judged false. Epiphanius was a monk from Palestine who became bishop of Salamis, the chief city and port of Cyprus. He named eighty heresies as poisons and set a cure beside each. Later writers, from Augustine to a council at Nicaea four centuries on, took his book as their list. In old age he carried the same fight to Jerusalem and Constantinople, against the followers of Origen, a Christian teacher who had died long before.</p><p>The historian Sozomen, writing later, says he was born near Eleutheropolis and chosen bishop by the people of Cyprus. He wrote the Panarion about 375, by his own dating. Each chapter names a heresy, tells its teaching and answers it. He knew some heresies from books, some by hearsay, some with his own eyes and ears. He copied one opponent&#039;s own letter whole, so that no one could say he answered from hearsay alone. He listed even the followers of Apollinaris, a man he says was always dear to him.</p><p>In the 390s he preached against Origen in the church at Jerusalem. The scholar Jerome, who was there and on his side, says the bishop of Jerusalem&#039;s party mocked him as an old fool. Yet crowds pressed to kiss his feet. In a letter to that bishop Epiphanius says why. Origen, he writes, taught that souls were angels who fell into bodies, denied that this flesh would rise again, and read the garden of Eden as hidden meanings. Epiphanius held to the plain sense. Genesis names the Euphrates among the rivers of paradise, and he writes: &quot;I have also drunk from the great river Euphrates. It was not spiritual water but real water, the kind you can touch with your hand and take into your mouth.&quot; If the rivers were real, so was Eden.</p><p>The same letter defends two acts of force. Jerome&#039;s brother had fled the priesthood several times. During a service near his monastery, he says, he had deacons seize the man and stop his mouth. Then he made him a deacon, then a priest, as the man cried that he was unworthy. In a village church he found a curtain painted with an image of Christ or a saint. He tore it apart and asked that such curtains be banned.</p><p>Then Theophilus, bishop of Alexandria, wrote to win him against Origen, though Theophilus had once accused Epiphanius of thinking God has a human form. Epiphanius had the bishops of Cyprus forbid reading Origen&#039;s books. The historian Socrates says Theophilus wanted revenge on monks he had driven out of Egypt, the Long Brothers, and used a man of simple mind and manners. Sozomen says instead that Epiphanius had long loathed Origen&#039;s books, and that Theophilus saw no danger in following his example, because the people praised him.</p><p>At Theophilus&#039;s urging, he sailed to Constantinople. There he ordained a deacon in the territory of its bishop, John Chrysostom, against church rules. He would not pray with John unless John condemned Origen&#039;s books and sent the Long Brothers away. Sozomen says one of those monks asked whether he had read any of their books. He had not, and had judged them by report. He sailed for Cyprus and died at sea before he reached home.</p><p>Augustine, who reports that Epiphanius set twenty heresies before Christ and sixty after, followed his order. Four centuries on, both sides in the quarrel over holy images claimed him. Opponents of images quoted writings under his name. In 787 the council at Nicaea ruled that the passage was not his, arguing that his eighty chapters never listed the making of images. A reply written at Charlemagne&#039;s order answered with his own curtain letter.</p><p>Epiphanius set one thing more after the eighty heresies in his chest of cures: the church, Christ&#039;s holy bride, which he says was there from the beginning.</p></div><p class="quotes-note">Quotations are given in our modern English. The printed wording of each is below.</p><details class="quotes-panel"><summary>Quotations and references</summary><p class="source-group">Quotations</p><ul class="sources-list"><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“We are going to show you the names of the heresies. We will uncover their unholy deeds, which are like venom and poison. Alongside them we will fit antidotes: cures for those who have been bitten, and protection for those who may yet fall into it.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“ἐπειδὴ γὰρ μέλλομεν ὑμῖν τά τε ὀνόματα τῶν αἱρέσεων δηλοῦν τάς τε παρ᾽ αὐτοῖς ἀϑεμίτους πράξεις ἀνακαλύπτειν, ὥσπερ ἰοὺς καὶ δηλητήρια ὄντα, σὺν αὐτοῖς δὲ ἅμα καὶ ἀντιδότους ἐφαρμόσαι, ἀλεξητήρια τῶν δεδηγμένων καὶ προκαταληπτικὰ τῶν μελλόντων εἰς τοῦτο ἐμπίπτειν”<br><span class="source-note">Epiphanius, Panarion, Proem I, Greek, ed. Karl Holl</span><br><span class="source-note">Holl&#039;s edition is the only print of this preface the Library holds, and its scan has slips inside the span: τε is read as TE in line 12388, καὶ as zal in line 12390, and κ as χ in προκαταληπτικὰ in line 12391. They are read here as the only words that fit. The English is the brief&#039;s own rendering of the Greek; no printed English is held.</span></li><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“I have also drunk from the great river Euphrates. It was not spiritual water but real water, the kind you can touch with your hand and take into your mouth.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“I have drunk also from the great river Euphrates, not spiritual but actual water, such as you can touch with your hand and imbibe with your mouth.”<br><span class="source-note">Epiphanius, letter to John, bishop of Jerusalem (Jerome&#039;s Letter 51), tr. W. H. Fremantle, in Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 6</span><br><span class="source-note">The Greek letter and Jerome&#039;s Latin of it are not held, so the English stands alone. The modern wording follows Fremantle&#039;s English.</span></li></ul><p class="source-group">References</p><ul class="sources-list"><li>Panarion, Proem I, Greek, ed. Holl: the name Panarion, the medicine chest, what the book is for, the eighty heresies, and the church set after them.</li><li>Panarion, Proem II: some heresies known from study, some by hearsay, some with his own ears and eyes; the date by the reigns of Valentinian, Valens and Gratian, which Holl&#039;s note reads as 375.</li><li>Panarion 33: Ptolemy&#039;s letter to Flora copied whole, so that no one could say he refuted it from hearsay alone.</li><li>Panarion 77: Apollinaris, always dear to him, and his followers listed.</li><li>Sozomen, Church History 6.32: his birth near Eleutheropolis and his choice as bishop by the people of Cyprus.</li><li>Jerome, Against John of Jerusalem 11: his sermon against Origen at Jerusalem, the mockery of the bishop&#039;s party, and the crowds pressing to kiss his feet.</li><li>Letter to John of Jerusalem (Jerome&#039;s Letter 51), tr. Fremantle: Origen&#039;s teaching on souls and the resurrection of this flesh, Eden read as hidden meanings, the rivers of Eden and the Euphrates; the seizing and ordination of Jerome&#039;s brother; the curtain at Anablatha.</li><li>Socrates, Church History 6.10, 6.12 and 6.14, and Sozomen, Church History 8.14 and 8.15: Theophilus and the ban in Cyprus, the voyage to Constantinople, the ordination in John&#039;s territory, the refusal to pray with John, the Long Brother&#039;s question, and the death at sea.</li><li>Augustine, On Heresies: Epiphanius&#039;s twenty heresies before Christ and sixty after, and his order followed.</li><li>The council of 787 and the Caroline Books, in Mendham&#039;s translation: the ruling that the passage against images was none of his, and the reply from his letter on the curtain.</li><li>World census, Early Palestinian Ascetic Monasticism: his tradition.</li></ul></details>
+    <div class="reading"><p>Epiphanius called his book the Panarion, which means a medicine chest. In its preface he says what it is for. &quot;We are going to show you the names of the heresies. We will uncover their unholy deeds, which are like venom and poison. Alongside them we will fit antidotes: cures for those who have been bitten, and protection for those who may yet fall into it.&quot;</p><p>A heresy, to Epiphanius, was a wrong belief held by people led astray ever since Adam, the first man. Epiphanius was a monk from Palestine who became bishop of Salamis, the chief city and port of Cyprus. He named eighty heresies as poisons and set a cure beside each. Later writers, from Augustine to a council at Nicaea four centuries after Epiphanius, took his book as their list. In old age he carried the same fight to Jerusalem and Constantinople, against the followers of Origen, a Christian teacher who had died long before.</p><p>He wrote the Panarion about 375. Each chapter names a heresy, tells its teaching and answers it, twenty from before Christ&#039;s coming and sixty after. He knew some heresies from books, some by report, some with his own eyes and ears. He copied one opponent&#039;s own letter whole, so that no one could say he answered from report alone.</p><p>He preached against Origen in the church at Jerusalem. In a letter to the bishop of Jerusalem Epiphanius says why. Origen, he writes, taught that souls were angels who fell into bodies, denied that this flesh would rise from the dead, and read the garden of Eden as hidden meanings. Epiphanius held to the plain sense. Genesis, the first book of the Bible, names the Euphrates among the rivers of Eden, and he writes: &quot;I have also drunk from the great river Euphrates. It was not spiritual water but real water, the kind you can touch with your hand and take into your mouth.&quot;</p><p>In the same letter he defends what he did to the scholar Jerome&#039;s brother, who had several times run away from being made a priest. During a service near Epiphanius&#039;s own monastery, he says, he had deacons, the clergy who serve under priests, seize the man and hold his mouth shut. Then he made him a deacon, then a priest, as the man cried that he was unworthy. The monks there had complained for a year that no one celebrated the sacraments, the church&#039;s holy rites, for them, he explains, and he feared they would drift from the church. In a village church he found a curtain painted with an image of Christ or a saint. Holding that an image of a man in church went against Scripture, he tore it apart and asked that such curtains be banned.</p><p>Then Theophilus, bishop of Alexandria, wrote to win him to his own campaign against Origen. His letter said he had driven monks who followed Origen out of Egypt. Epiphanius had the bishops of Cyprus forbid reading Origen&#039;s books. The historian Socrates says Theophilus wanted revenge on those monks, the Long Brothers, and used Epiphanius, a man so devout that he was simple in mind and manners. Another historian, Sozomen, says instead that Epiphanius had long loathed Origen&#039;s books, and that Theophilus saw no danger in following his example, because the people praised Epiphanius.</p><p>At Theophilus&#039;s urging, he sailed to Constantinople. There he made a man a deacon in the territory of its bishop, John Chrysostom, against church rules. He would not pray with John unless John condemned Origen&#039;s books and drove the Long Brothers from the city. One of the Long Brothers asked him whether he had read any of their books. He had not, and had judged them by report. He sailed for Cyprus and died at sea before he reached home.</p><p>Augustine wrote his own book on the heresies in the order of Epiphanius&#039;s list. Four centuries on, when Christians quarreled over pictures of Christ and the saints in churches, both sides claimed him, and opponents of images quoted writings under his name. In 787 the council at Nicaea ruled that those writings were not his, arguing that his eighty chapters never listed the making of images. A book written to answer the council&#039;s argument replied with his own letter about the torn curtain.</p><p>Epiphanius set one thing more after the eighty heresies in his chest of cures: the church, which he calls Christ&#039;s holy bride and says was there from the beginning.</p></div><p class="quotes-note">Quotations are given in our modern English. The printed wording of each is below.</p><details class="quotes-panel"><summary>Quotations and references</summary><p class="source-group">Quotations</p><ul class="sources-list"><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“We are going to show you the names of the heresies. We will uncover their unholy deeds, which are like venom and poison. Alongside them we will fit antidotes: cures for those who have been bitten, and protection for those who may yet fall into it.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“ἐπειδὴ γὰρ μέλλομεν ὑμῖν τά τε ὀνόματα τῶν αἱρέσεων δηλοῦν τάς τε παρ᾽ αὐτοῖς ἀϑεμίτους πράξεις ἀνακαλύπτειν, ὥσπερ ἰοὺς καὶ δηλητήρια ὄντα, σὺν αὐτοῖς δὲ ἅμα καὶ ἀντιδότους ἐφαρμόσαι, ἀλεξητήρια τῶν δεδηγμένων καὶ προκαταληπτικὰ τῶν μελλόντων εἰς τοῦτο ἐμπίπτειν”<br><span class="source-note">Epiphanius, Panarion, Proem I, Greek, ed. Karl Holl</span><br><span class="source-note">Holl&#039;s edition is the only print of this preface the Library holds, and its scan has slips inside the span: τε is read as TE in line 12388, καὶ as zal in line 12390, and κ as χ in προκαταληπτικὰ in line 12391. They are read here as the only words that fit. The English is the brief&#039;s own rendering of the Greek; no printed English is held.</span></li><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“I have also drunk from the great river Euphrates. It was not spiritual water but real water, the kind you can touch with your hand and take into your mouth.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“I have drunk also from the great river Euphrates, not spiritual but actual water, such as you can touch with your hand and imbibe with your mouth.”<br><span class="source-note">Epiphanius, letter to John, bishop of Jerusalem (Jerome&#039;s Letter 51), tr. W. H. Fremantle, in Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 6</span><br><span class="source-note">The Greek letter and Jerome&#039;s Latin of it are not held, so the English stands alone. The modern wording follows Fremantle&#039;s English.</span></li></ul><p class="source-group">References</p><ul class="sources-list"><li>Panarion, Proem I, Greek, ed. Holl: the name Panarion, the medicine chest, what the book is for, the eighty heresies, and the church set after them.</li><li>Panarion, Proem I, the list of the heresies: twenty before Christ&#039;s coming and sixty after.</li><li>Panarion, Proem II: a heresy as a wrong belief spread by people led astray since man was formed on earth; some heresies known from study, some by hearsay, some with his own ears and eyes; the date by the reigns of Valentinian, Valens and Gratian, which Holl&#039;s note reads as 375.</li><li>Panarion 33: Ptolemy&#039;s letter to Flora copied whole, so that no one could say he refuted it from hearsay alone.</li><li>Sozomen, Church History 6.32: a monk in Palestine and Egypt, chosen bishop of the chief city of Cyprus, a seaport.</li><li>Jerome, Against John of Jerusalem 11: his sermon against Origen in the church at Jerusalem.</li><li>Letter to John of Jerusalem (Jerome&#039;s Letter 51), tr. Fremantle: Origen&#039;s teaching on souls and the resurrection of this flesh, Eden read as hidden meanings, the rivers of Eden and the Euphrates; the seizing and ordination of Jerome&#039;s brother, with his reasons, the monks&#039; year of complaint that no one celebrated the sacraments for them and his fear that they would be turned from the Church; the curtain at Anablatha, and his reason, that an image of a man in Christ&#039;s church went against the Scriptures.</li><li>Theophilus&#039;s letter to Epiphanius (Jerome&#039;s Letter 90), tr. Fremantle: the monks who followed Origen cut down and driven from Egypt, and the call to gather the bishops of Cyprus.</li><li>Socrates, Church History 6.10, 6.12 and 6.14, and Sozomen, Church History 8.14 and 8.15: Theophilus and the ban in Cyprus; Socrates on Theophilus&#039;s revenge on the monks he had driven out and on Epiphanius&#039;s piety and simple mind and manners, Origen dead long before; Sozomen on Epiphanius&#039;s long aversion to Origen&#039;s books and Theophilus following his example because the people praised him; the voyage to Constantinople, the ordination in John&#039;s territory, the refusal to pray with John, the Long Brother&#039;s question, and the death at sea.</li><li>Augustine, On Heresies: his order followed.</li><li>The council of 787 and the Caroline Books, in Mendham&#039;s translation: the quarrel over images of Christ and the saints in churches; the writings against images quoted under his name; the ruling that they were none of his, since his eighty chapters list no heresy of making images; and the reply from his letter on the curtain.</li><li>World census, Early Palestinian Ascetic Monasticism: his tradition.</li></ul></details>
