-  <section><h2>Key Works</h2><ul><li><b>Panarion</b>: his book against the heresies, which Jerome calls &quot;Against all heresies&quot;; heresies 1 to 80 are held in Greek</li><li><b>Ancoratus</b>: &quot;The Anchored&quot;, which the monk Ammonius says in Sozomen&#039;s account he had read often; held in Greek</li><li><b>Letter to John, bishop of Jerusalem</b>: his defense of the ordination of Paulinian and his charges against Origen, held in Jerome&#039;s Latin version, in English</li></ul></section><section><h2>Legacy</h2><h3>What traces to them</h3><ul><li>The Panarion&#039;s account of the heresies. It keeps writings of some of those it attacks, among them the propositions of Aetius of Antioch, quoted in heresy 76.</li><li>The condemnation of Origen&#039;s books in Cyprus, which Socrates of Constantinople and Sozomen say he decreed at a synod there.</li><li>Augustine, in On Heresies, written about 428 by its editors&#039; date, says that in listing the heretics he followed Epiphanius&#039;s order but not his measure. He says he took fifty-seven heresies from Epiphanius&#039;s own work, and that among the Greeks Epiphanius was counted among the great.</li><li>Photius, a patriarch of Constantinople in the ninth century, judged that Epiphanius wrote more fully and effectively against heretics than any of his predecessors, and that his style is poor.</li><li>Jerome says Epiphanius, who had much intercourse with Hilarion, set out Hilarion&#039;s praises in a short letter that was widely circulated. The letter is not held.</li></ul><h3>How they're remembered</h3><ul><li>Sozomen calls him &quot;the most revered man under the whole heaven&quot;, and says it was reported that demons were driven out and diseases healed at his tomb.</li><li>Jerome says his books were read by the learned for their matter, &quot;and also by the plain people, on account of their language.&quot;</li><li>Socrates of Constantinople says that &quot;on account of his extraordinary piety&quot; he was &quot;a man of simple mind and manners&quot;, and that Theophilus of Alexandria used him.</li><li>Sozomen says he was instructed from his youth by the most celebrated ascetics and for that reason passed most of his time in Egypt.</li><li>Sozomen says he was extremely liberal to the needy, among them those who had suffered shipwreck, and that after spending his whole inheritance on them he applied the treasures of the church to the same purpose.</li><li>Jerome says monks came to Cyprus from all parts of the world for love of him.</li><li>The Dialogue on the life of Chrysostom says he ruled the church in Cyprus for thirty-six years.</li><li>Theophilus of Alexandria, in a letter in Jerome&#039;s Latin, says he &quot;has shone throughout all the world a bright star among bishops&quot;.</li></ul></section><section><h2>What Is Uncertain</h2><ul><li>Epiphanius wrote against the heresies as their enemy, and his reports of them are a foe&#039;s.</li><li>Socrates and Sozomen report his last words to John Chrysostom, and John&#039;s answer, as hearsay. Socrates says he cannot be sure his informants spoke the truth.</li><li>Theophilus of Alexandria once accused him of thinking that God has a human form, Socrates says. A. C. Zenos, in a note to his translation of Socrates, says Epiphanius himself rejected that view, on Jerome&#039;s testimony.</li><li>Theophilus&#039;s own judgment of him changed. The Dialogue on the life of Chrysostom, which Herbert Moore gives to Palladius of Helenopolis, says Theophilus first called him &quot;a heretic, or a paltry schismatic&quot;, and later &quot;a most holy saint&quot;.</li><li>His dates are Richardson&#039;s reckoning. No held ancient source gives the year of his birth.</li><li>The Library holds the Panarion in Greek from heresy 1 on, and the Ancoratus in Greek.</li><li>The books of the Panarion are counted three ways. His own preface says three. Photius says three volumes holding seven books. Augustine says Epiphanius made six books