     <h2>About This Person</h2>
     
-    <div class="reading"><p>In 399 the bishop of Constantinople, the eastern capital, was John, a preacher from Antioch. Not long before, the emperor&#039;s chief eunuch, Eutropius, had a law passed that stripped refugees of the right to shelter in churches. Then he fell from favor and fled into the cathedral. As he lay trembling under the altar, John climbed into the pulpit and preached against him. Socrates of Constantinople says some thought John added insult to cruelty. John said he spoke to soften the people&#039;s hearts toward him, for the church, like a mother, had hidden her enemy under her cloak.</p><p>He preached plainly about the poor, and his blunt words to the powerful led to his exile. At Antioch John had trained under Diodore, who kept to the literal sense of Scripture, in the Antiochene way. There, in a sermon on Matthew, he told his listeners that if they wanted to honor the body of Christ, they must not neglect Him when He is naked.</p><p>In 398 Eutropius made Theophilus of Alexandria ordain him. Palladius, who stood by John to the end, says he moved the bishop&#039;s extravagant spending to the hospital.</p><p>Socrates says his rebukes of men in office turned the tide against him. It is said he was as blunt with the Gothic general Gainas, who asked for a city church for the Arians. They taught that the Son of God had a beginning. Socrates says Theophilus held a grudge, since his favorite had lost the see to John. Monks Theophilus had driven out of Egypt, in a quarrel over whether God has a human body, came to John for help. Socrates says that when Theophilus heard, falsely, that John had given them the Eucharist, he resolved to unseat him.</p><p>In 403 he and his bishops, meeting at the Oak near Chalcedon, cited John to appear. His accusers charged that he had sold church property, insulted the clergy, struck a man and eaten greedily alone. When John refused them as his enemies, they deposed him, Socrates says, for refusing. The people rose, and he was recalled.</p><p>Public games were held at a silver statue of the empress Eudoxia beside the cathedral. John mocked those who allowed them, and she took this as aimed at her. A second council silenced him for taking back his see without a council&#039;s leave, and in 404 the emperor sent him into exile. In 407, Palladius says, soldiers hurried him toward Pityus, hoping he would die. He died on the way, with his usual words, &quot;Glory to God for all things.&quot;</p><p>In 438 Bishop Proclus carried John&#039;s body back to Constantinople. By this, Socrates says, he brought back to the church&#039;s communion the followers who had left it over John&#039;s deposition.</p></div><p class="quotes-note">Quotations are given in our modern English. The printed wording of each is below.</p><details class="quotes-panel"><summary>Quotations and references</summary><p class="source-group">Quotations</p><ul class="sources-list"><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“Glory to God for all things.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“Glory to God for all things;”<br><span class="source-note">Palladius, Dialogue on the Life of Chrysostom, tr. Moore</span></li></ul><p class="source-group">References</p><ul class="sources-list"><li>Socrates, Church History VI.3, tr. A. C. Zenos: John a native of Antioch.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.5, tr. Zenos: Eutropius&#039;s law against sanctuary, his fall and flight into the cathedral, trembling under the altar table; John&#039;s sermon, which some thought added insult to cruelty.</li><li>John Chrysostom, Homily I on Eutropius: John&#039;s own account, that he spoke to soften hearts toward Eutropius, the church like a mother hiding her enemy under her cloak.</li><li>Sozomen, Church History VIII.2, tr. Hartranft: John&#039;s training under Diodore. Socrates, Church History VI.3, tr. Zenos: Diodore&#039;s literal sense of Scripture.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.2, tr. Zenos: Eutropius making Theophilus of Alexandria ordain John in 398.</li><li>Palladius, Dialogue on the Life of Chrysostom, tr. Herbert Moore: the bishop&#039;s spending moved to the hospital.</li><li>John Chrysostom, Homily 50 on Matthew, tr. George Prevost: honor Christ&#039;s body, and do not neglect Him when He is naked.</li><li>Homilies on Matthew, Preface to the Oxford Edition, by C. Marriott: the homilies were delivered at Antioch.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.5, tr. Zenos: his rebukes of men in office turning the tide against him; Theophilus&#039;s grudge over his favorite.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.5, tr. Zenos: John&#039;s rebukes reaching the Gothic general Gainas, who asked for a church in the city for the Arians, told with Socrates&#039;s &quot;it is said&quot;. Socrates, Church History I.5, tr. Zenos: the Arian teaching that the Son had a beginning.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.7 and VI.9, tr. Zenos: the Egyptian monks driven out by Theophilus in a quarrel over whether God has a human form; their coming to John at Constantinople; the false report that reached Theophilus that John had admitted them to the mysteries, and his resolve to unseat John.</li><li>Photius, Library, Codex 59, tr. J. H. Freese: the charges laid against John at the Oak, among them that he sold much valuable church property, reviled the clergy, struck Memnon with his fist and ate gluttonously alone.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.15, tr. Zenos: the Oak near Chalcedon in 403, John refusing judges who were his enemies, and his deposition for refusing.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.15-16, tr. Zenos: the people rising and John&#039;s recall.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.18, tr. Zenos: the silver statue of Eudoxia beside the cathedral, the games held at it, John&#039;s mockery of those who allowed them, and her taking his words as aimed at her; the second council&#039;s single ground, that he took back his see after his deposition without an ecclesiastical council&#039;s authority; the emperor&#039;s decree of exile.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.18, tr. Zenos: the clash with Eudoxia, the second council that silenced him, the decree of exile in 404.</li><li>Palladius, Dialogue on the Life of Chrysostom, tr. Moore: in 407 soldiers hurrying him toward Pityus; his death on the way, using his customary formula, &quot;Glory to God for all things&quot;.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VII.45, tr. Zenos: Proclus carrying John&#039;s body back to Constantinople in 438, and by this winning back the followers who had left the church&#039;s communion.</li><li>World census, Antiochene Exegetical Christianity (Chrysostom-centered): his tradition.</li></ul></details>
+    <div class="reading"><p>The emperor&#039;s chief chamberlain, Eutropius, had a law passed that stripped people who fled into churches of the right to shelter there. Soon after, in 399, he fell from favor and himself fled into the cathedral of Constantinople, the emperor&#039;s city. As he lay trembling under the altar, the bishop, John Chrysostom, climbed into the pulpit and preached against him. Some thought he was attacking a man already brought low. John said he spoke to soften the people&#039;s hearts, for the church had sheltered its enemy: &quot;When everyone ignored him in his desolation, she alone, like a loving mother, hid him behind her curtains. She stood against the emperor&#039;s anger, the people&#039;s fury and unbearable hatred.&quot;</p><p>He preached plainly about the poor, and his blunt words to the powerful led to his exile. His sermons were read for centuries, and the followers who left the church over his fall came back only when his body came home.</p><p>John was born at Antioch, in Syria, and as a priest there for twelve years he preached on what the rich owed the poor. They decked the altar, where the bread of Communion lies, which Christians call Christ&#039;s body, with gold cups and silk. In one sermon he told them to honor Christ in the poor first: &quot;Do you want to honor Christ&#039;s body? Do not neglect him when he is naked. While you honor him here with silk clothes, do not neglect him outside, dying of cold and nakedness.&quot; He published some of his sermons, and shorthand writers took down others as he spoke. Both kinds were kept and read.</p><p>In 398 the court chose him as bishop of Constantinople, and Eutropius made Theophilus, bishop of Alexandria, ordain him. As bishop he cut the bishop&#039;s own spending and gave the money to a hospital. He corrected his clergy sharply over how they lived and put some of them out of the church, and many came to hate him.</p><p>When a Gothic general asked for a church inside the city for the Arians, who taught that the Son of God had a beginning, John spoke against it before the emperor. Socrates, a church historian of the city writing a generation later, says Theophilus also held a grudge, since his own choice for bishop had lost out to John, and that he resolved to unseat him.</p><p>Theophilus and his bishops met at a place called the Oak, outside Chalcedon, and summoned John. His accusers charged that he had sold church property, insulted the clergy, struck a man and eaten greedily alone. John refused to come before judges he called his enemies, and asked for a council of the whole church. Socrates says they removed him from office only for refusing to come. A later bishop of Constantinople summed up the meeting&#039;s own written record, and they tell it differently: some charges were heard, and then all forty-five bishops voted to remove him. The emperor ordered him into exile, and John gave himself up, but he was soon called back.</p><p>Public games were held at a silver statue of the empress Eudoxia, just across the street from the cathedral. John mocked those who allowed them, calling the games an insult to the church, and the empress took this as aimed at her and sought another council. It condemned him for taking back his place as bishop, after his removal, without a council&#039;s leave. Since two councils had condemned him, the emperor barred him from the church, and John was silenced. His followers kept Easter in the public baths, and from then on they met apart from the church. In 404 he was taken into exile by force.</p><p>He was first exiled to Armenia. Palladius, who wrote John&#039;s life and took his side, says bishops in Syria, seeing the church of Antioch still looking to John, had him moved to Pityus, a more distant place on the Black Sea. In his telling, the soldiers hurried John on, saying they had been told his death would bring them promotion. Near Comana he asked for white clothes, took Communion and prayed the words he always used: &quot;Glory to God for all things.&quot; He died there, in September 407.</p><p>In 438 the bishop of Constantinople had the emperor&#039;s leave to bring John&#039;s body home from Comana. He carried it through the city in procession and laid it in the Church of the Apostles, and those who had left the church over John&#039;s fall came back.</p></div><p class="quotes-note">Quotations are given in our modern English. The printed wording of each is below.</p><details class="quotes-panel"><summary>Quotations and references</summary><p class="source-group">Quotations</p><ul class="sources-list"><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“When everyone ignored him in his desolation, she alone, like a loving mother, hid him behind her curtains. She stood against the emperor&#039;s anger, the people&#039;s fury and unbearable hatred.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“when all have despised him in his desolation, she alone like an affectionate mother has concealed him under her cloak, opposing both the wrath of the king, and the rage of the people, and their overwhelming hatred.”<br><span class="source-note">John Chrysostom, Homily I on Eutropius, tr. W. R. W. Stephens</span><br><span class="source-note">The modern English is rendered from the Greek, Patrologia Graeca 52, lines 822-827 of its file, the only Greek print held. Its scan misreads several letters, so the English print stands here. The Greek has her curtains where Stephens has her cloak.</span></li><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“Do you want to honor Christ&#039;s body? Do not neglect him when he is naked. While you honor him here with silk clothes, do not neglect him outside, dying of cold and nakedness.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“Wouldest thou do honor to Christ’s body? Neglect Him not when naked; do not while here thou honorest Him with silken garments, neglect Him perishing without of cold and nakedness.”<br><span class="source-note">John Chrysostom, Homily 50 on Matthew, tr. George Prevost, rev. M. B. Riddle</span><br><span class="source-note">The Greek of this homily is not held; the modern English is rendered from Prevost&#039;s.</span></li><li><span class="source-type">In our modern English</span><br>“Glory to God for all things.”<br><span class="source-type">As printed</span><br>“Glory to God for all things;”<br><span class="source-note">Palladius, Dialogue on the Life of Chrysostom, tr. Moore</span><br><span class="source-note">The Greek is kept as the scan prints it: πάγτων ἔγεχεν stands for πάντων ἕνεκεν. The Life under the name of Theodore of Trimithus prints the same words at lines 5813-5814 of the same file.</span></li></ul><p class="source-group">References</p><ul class="sources-list"><li>Socrates, Church History VI.5, tr. Zenos: Eutropius&#039;s law against sanctuary, his fall in his own consular year, 399, and flight into the cathedral, trembling under the altar table; John&#039;s sermon, which some thought added insult to cruelty.</li><li>John Chrysostom, Homily I on Eutropius, Greek in Patrologia Graeca 52, tr. W. R. W. Stephens: John&#039;s own account, that he spoke to soften hearts toward Eutropius, and the church like a loving mother hiding him behind her curtains, against the emperor&#039;s anger, the people&#039;s fury and their hatred.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.5, tr. Zenos: his rebukes of men in office turning the tide against him; Theophilus&#039;s grudge over his favorite.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.4, tr. Zenos: his sermons, some published by himself and others taken down by shorthand writers as he spoke.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VII.45, tr. Zenos: the followers who had left the church over his deposition brought back when his body came home.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.3, tr. A. C. Zenos: John a native of Antioch.</li><li>Palladius, Dialogue on the Life of Chrysostom, tr. Moore: twelve years a priest