<li>Socrates, Church History VI.15-16, tr. Zenos: the emperor&#039;s decree of exile, John giving himself up, and his recall.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.18, tr. Zenos: the silver statue of Eudoxia beside the cathedral, the games held at it, John&#039;s mockery of those who allowed them, and her taking his words as aimed at her; the second council&#039;s single ground, that he took back his see after his deposition without an ecclesiastical council&#039;s authority; the emperor&#039;s decree of exile.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.18, tr. Zenos: the emperor&#039;s word that he could not go to the church because two synods had condemned him; his silence; his followers keeping Easter in the public baths and from then on meeting apart; his exile by force.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.18, tr. Zenos: the clash with Eudoxia, the second council that silenced him, the decree of exile in 404.</li><li>Sozomen, Church History VIII.22, tr. Hartranft: his first exile at Cucusus in Armenia.</li><li>Palladius, Dialogue on the Life of Chrysostom, tr. Moore: the bishops of Syria, seeing the church of Antioch turning to him in Armenia, having him moved to Pityus on the Black Sea; the soldiers hurrying him on, saying his death would bring them promotion; near Comana his white clothes, Communion and death, using his customary formula, &quot;Glory to God for all things&quot;. The same words in the Dialogue&#039;s Greek and in the Life under the name of Theodore of Trimithus, Patrologia Graeca 47.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VI.21, tr. Zenos: his death on the fourteenth of September, 407.</li><li>Socrates, Church History VII.45, tr. Zenos: Proclus, with the emperor&#039;s leave, carrying John&#039;s body from Comana to Constantinople in 438, in procession through the city to the Church of the Apostles, and by this winning back the followers who had left the church&#039;s communion. The history ends with these years, a generation after John.</li><li>World census,